    - target: {fileID: 858565852227398588, guid: 10e0d6b75c2974f419b725ca2082b7b0, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: '<b>Navsarika</b> - During the 7th and 8th centuries, the Chalukya Dynasty
        ruled the region. They called the area <b>Navasarika</b> in their official
        records and used it as a regional capital.



        <b>Nagshahi & Nag Mandal</b>:
        This name came from ancient snake-worship customs and a local legend. According
        to folklore, a sacred cobra guarded a pond in Navsari and allowed only true
        worshippers to approach the water.



        <b>Nagsharak</b>: This poetic
        name honored a story about an unlikely friendship. It described a cobra (Nag)
        and a Mynah bird (Sarika) that lived peacefully together and danced in an
        old Pipal tree in Navsari.



        <b>Navsareh</b>: This variation came
        from one of two sources. It either meant <b>Nav Sahara</b> ("New Abode"),
        named by arriving Muslim settlers, or referred to the nine original settlements
        (sareh) that formed the land.



        <b>Navsari</b>: Parsi refugees fleeing
        Persia settled here in 1142 CE. The location reminded them of their home
        region, Sari, in northern Iran. In Persian, nav meant "new," so they named
        their new home <b>Nao-Sari</b> ("New Sari").'
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 1628190776190876986, guid: 10e0d6b75c2974f419b725ca2082b7b0, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_Name
      value: SwipePage (1)
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 1628190776190876986, guid: 10e0d6b75c2974f419b725ca2082b7b0, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_IsActive
      value: 1
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5628298207313465921, guid: 10e0d6b75c2974f419b725ca2082b7b0, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: "The name Navsari didn't \nalways exist"
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
