    - target: {fileID: 858565852227398588, guid: 10e0d6b75c2974f419b725ca2082b7b0, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_text
      value: "For centuries, Navsari has been the spiritual heart of India's Zoroastrian
        community. After the Parsis migrated from Persia, it emerged as the foremost
        centre of the Parsi priesthood.\r\n\r\nUnlike other early Parsi settlements,
        which experienced periods of evacuation or abandonment, Navsari is the only
        one with an unbroken history.\r\n\r\n- <b>1142 CE</b> India's oldest continuously
        functioning fire temple, the Vadi Dar-e-Meher, was founded in Navsari. For
        more than 800 years, it has served as a premier training ground for Parsi
        priests and a centre of religious learning.\r\n\r\n- <b>15th century</b>
        Navsari's importance grew further when the Iranshah Atash Behram, the holiest
        fire in Zoroastrianism, was brought from Sanjan for safekeeping. The Sanjana
        and Bhagaria priestly families cared for the sacred fire here for over 250
        years before it was moved to Udvada.\r\n\r\n- <b>Until the 1970s</b> Navsari
        remained the principal ritual centre of the Parsi community, where priests
        received advanced training and consecrated ritual requisites (\u0101l\u0101t)
        were prepared for communities across western India.\r\n\r\nBy preserving
        sacred traditions and nurturing generations of priests, Navsari has earned
        its enduring legacy as the Parsis' Dharam-ni-Tekdi:the spiritual centre of
        Zoroastrianism in India."
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 1628190776190876986, guid: 10e0d6b75c2974f419b725ca2082b7b0, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_Name
      value: SwipePage (2)
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 1628190776190876986, guid: 10e0d6b75c2974f419b725ca2082b7b0, type: 3}
      propertyPath: m_IsActive
